WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.600
Speaker 1: Republicans plan to force candidates
who want to debate in the 2024 primary to

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:15.129
sign a loyalty pledge clearly here geared
at getting Donald Trump to say he will support

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:16.129
whoever the eventual.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:17.320
Speaker 2: Nominee is going to be.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:23.539
Speaker 1: And this is the latest in an ill
conceived and potentially completely self-destructive

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:25.849
idea from the Republican National Committee.

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:27.310
Speaker 2: I will explain why.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.359
Speaker 1: Let's talk about these loyalty
pledges.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:35.590
The Associated Press reports Republican primary
candidates will be blocked from the debate

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:43.950
stage this summer if they do not sign a pledge
to support the GOP's ultimate presidential

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.579
nominee, according to draft language set to
be adopted when the RNC.

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:50.280
Speaker 2: Meets next week.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.800
Speaker 1: The proposal sets up a potential
clash with former President Trump, who has

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.730
raised the possibility of leaving the Republican
Party and launching an independent candidacy.

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.270
Speaker 2: If he doesn't win the nomination.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:05.950
Speaker 1: While RNC officials and Trump aides
downplayed that possibility, such a move could

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:11.590
destroy the Republican Party's White House
aspirations in 2024 and raise existential.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.670
Speaker 2: Questions about the party's future.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.390
Speaker 1: This is so potentially disastrous
for.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.070
Speaker 2: The Republican Party, and I love
it.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:28.860
Speaker 1: Now, interestingly, a senior Trump
aide could not say whether Trump would sign

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.049
the pledge to support the.

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:31.049
Speaker 2: Eventual nominee.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:34.840
Speaker 1: But suggested privately that he
plans to participate.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.070
Speaker 2: In the debates.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:40.530
Speaker 1: Campaign spokesman Steven Chung
declined to answer the question.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:41.530
Speaker 2: Directly as while.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.159
Speaker 1: Chung said President Trump is the
undisputed leader of the.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:45.840
Speaker 2: Republican Party and will be the
nominee.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.640
Speaker 1: There is nobody who can outmatch
President Trump's energy or the enthusiasm

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.640
he received from Americans.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.640
Speaker 2: Of all backgrounds.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:57.130
Speaker 1: So listen, I don't think Trump
can do this.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.310
I don't think Trump can sign that loyalty
pledge.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.340
Speaker 2: And there's a couple different
reasons why.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:07.409
Speaker 1: First of all, existentially, Trump
may not win the nomination.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:08.700
We just don't know yet.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.849
If you put him head to head up against the
Santas, it's not that good.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.580
If you put him in a multi-way race, he has
a plurality.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.580
But that could change.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.900
And what if he gets indicted or whatever the
case may be?

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:20.910
There's all these different scenarios.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:26.780
So Trump, in order to maintain leverage in
power, really can't sign this pledge because

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:29.390
he kneecaps his own power.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:37.980
Part of the power of Trump right now is that
if he chooses to go and run third party, he

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.060
ends Republican.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.150
Speaker 2: Chances in 2024.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.720
Speaker 1: The math of it is relatively simple.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.050
We've talked about it before.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:49.550
You think about all of these states which
are swing states or they're Republican states

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:51.810
by but only by a few points.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:57.850
If you take a state that Republicans usually
win by three, Trump saying, I'm running third

00:02:57.850 --> 00:02:59.140
party.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:05.480
If he pulls even just ten or 12% of the vote
in those states, it'll mostly come off of

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.480
the Republican.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.400
Speaker 2: Nominee and the Democrat ends up.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.400
Speaker 1: Winning.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.310
Trump running third party is a gift to Democrats
if it happens.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.300
I would love to see it.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:18.500
He would he would have the chance at being
one of the most successful third party candidates

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.500
in history.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:26.440
I mean, in the modern era, you've got Ross
Perot is the one that mostly comes to mind.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.670
Trump would really have a shot at being one
of the more successful third party candidates

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.030
in the modern era, and it would destroy Republicans.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.030
Speaker 2: Possibility of winning.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.379
So that would be absolutely fantastic.

00:03:34.379 --> 00:03:43.450
Speaker 1: But there's another sort of aspect
to this, which is much of Trump's campaign,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.760
like in 2016, is going to be based on attacking
his fellow.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.810
Speaker 2: Republicans and making fun of them
and talking about how terrible they are and

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:52.900
all these different things.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:59.120
Speaker 1: How can Trump seriously attack
Ron DeSantis in the way he's been attacking

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:05.799
him if he has also signed on to support to
Santos no matter what, if DeSantis is the

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:06.840
nominee?

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.170
It fundamentally doesn't work.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:14.909
And so the loyalty pledge actually risks pushing
Trump towards.

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:17.320
Speaker 2: That third party run in the following
way.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.139
Speaker 1: If Republicans if the Republican
Party really holds firm and says Trump.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:24.560
Speaker 2: If you don't sign this thing, you
can't debate.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.570
By the way, not allowing a former president
to debate would be unbelievable.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:31.310
And many people are writing to me saying,
David, they would just buckle.

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.560
They would just say, oh, no, no, no, you're
allowed to debate.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:37.180
Speaker 1: But imagine that the Republican
Party says you've got to sign this loyalty

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.130
pledge in order to debate.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.130
Trump doesn't sign it.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.630
Speaker 2: They say, we're not letting you
debate.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:46.419
Speaker 1: That pushes Trump to go and say,
you won't let me debate.

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.380
I'm no longer running in your primary.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.010
Speaker 2: I'm now running third party.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.010
That's it.

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:53.090
Democrat wins in 2024.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:59.470
Speaker 1: So Trump really does have a very
powerful tool in his pocket.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:00.470
And he can.

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:01.470
And use it like a cudgel.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.120
He genuinely can.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.699
It'll be fascinating to.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:05.699
Speaker 2: See.

00:05:05.699 --> 00:05:06.050
Speaker 1: If it happens.

